WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:05.750
The gorgeous sun-drenched world of Luca is
overflowing with Pixar easter eggs, from Pizza

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:10.490
Planet trucks, to Luxo Balls, deep-sea Finding
Nemo references, and hat-tips to Up.

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:15.170
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing over 80 easter eggs

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:19.220
and crucial details plus the post-credits
scene you might have missed in Luca.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.119
Some mild spoilers ahead, so take care.

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:27.190
Italian food features heavily in Luca from
the enormous sandwiches that town bully Ercole

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:30.410
enjoys, to the pasta eating section of the
Portorosso Cup;

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:35.500
however, Pixar's nod to its legendary Pizza
Planet truck is much harder to find.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.760
So, watch carefully during the final part
of the bicycle race towards the end of the

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.860
movie and it appears very briefly just behind
Ercole as he's chasing Luca and Alberto down

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.969
the cobbled streets.

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:51.260
Another tricky-to-spot regular Pixar easter
egg is the famous Luxo ball.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.149
Look out for the ball's signature yellow,
red and blue colours during an aerial shot

00:00:55.149 --> 00:00:57.300
of the bicycle race.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.510
Another recurring Pixar easter egg, A113,
appears on the train ticket that Alberto gives

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:05.010
to Luca so he can go to school in Genoa.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:09.970
The appearance of A113 in Pixar's films is
a tribute to the classroom at California Institute

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.680
of the Arts where many of the studio's animators
studied.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.190
And the number on the carriage that Luca boards
"1200 PA" is a reference to the address of

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:22.110
Pixar's headquarters:
1200 Park Avenue, Emeryville.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:27.210
And the number on the front of the train,
94608, is the studio's zip code.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:31.390
Hidden in one of the signs for soft drinks
in the town is a deep-cut reference to one

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.080
of Pixar's most beloved films, Up.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.610
This poster on a side street off the main
square has an ad for a cherry-flavoured drink,

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.670
with various bottle caps on the right for
other flavours.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.460
It's a little difficult to see but, the purple-coloured
cap is a nod to the grape soda pin that Ellie

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:48.180
gave Carl when they were kids in Up.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.500
"You and me, we're in a club now."

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.710
The Art of Luca book shows a design was made
for a bottle cap labelled "Uva", the Italian

00:01:55.710 --> 00:02:00.300
word for grape, confirming this is indeed
a reference to Up.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.170
During one of Luca's dream sequences, as he
soars through the air on a Leonardo Da Vinci

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:09.270
flying machine, below him is the famous fictional
wooden marionette, Pinocchio, together with

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:13.819
the cat and the fox from the original Italian
book, which many decades later was adapted

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:15.430
for Disney's animated movie.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:20.040
And there's another Pinocchio reference in
the song "Il Gatto e la Volpe", the Cat and

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.680
the Fox, which plays as Alberto and Luca build
another homemade Vespa.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:29.099
The cat and the fox references the two rogues
from the story of Pinocchio, and director

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:34.240
Enrico Casarosa chose the song not only for
its playful and fun feel, but also because

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.900
he sees similarities between Luca and Alberto
and the cat and the fox, and how each pair

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.930
gets into trouble together.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:45.139
There's also a Pinocchio figurine in Giulia's
bedroom, and the story of the famous wooden

00:02:45.139 --> 00:02:50.340
puppet who eventually becomes a real human
boy also has some parallels with Luca's story

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.069
as he can transform from a sea monster into
a human.

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:58.079
Another intriguing easter egg I spotted in
Giulia's bedroom is a record cover for an

00:02:58.079 --> 00:03:03.400
artist called Nicolo Pitera, a reference to
Nick Pitera who's an animator for Pixar and

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.769
also one of the voices in the TripleDent Gum
jingle from Inside Out.

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:09.680
"TripleDent gum..
..will make you smile."

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.049
"Did I ask for the gum commercial?!"

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:15.859
Nick Pitera also has his own YouTube channel
where he covers numerous songs, including

00:03:15.859 --> 00:03:20.140
many from Disney musicals and Remember Me
from Pixar's Coco.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.439
In Giulia's bedroom, there's a cute Donald
Duck toy, and some of these cloud formations

00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:28.480
that appear in one of Luca's dream sequences
look like hidden mickeys.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.019
Another Disney reference springs up on one
of the film posters in the town square, for

00:03:33.019 --> 00:03:37.709
the 1954 movie "20,000 Leagues Under The Sea",
the underwater adventure adapted from the

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:38.950
book by Jules Verne.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:43.790
Verne was also briefly referenced in another
Pixar creature feature, Monsters Inc., via

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:48.219
a poster in a Parisian room that Mike, Sully
and Boo fall into.

00:03:48.219 --> 00:03:53.379
There's also a fun fictional movie poster
for a film called "Attacco del Mostro Marino",

00:03:53.379 --> 00:03:57.439
Attack of the Sea Monster, which features
a fearsome creature that looks a lot like

00:03:57.439 --> 00:03:58.559
Uncle Ugo.

00:03:58.559 --> 00:04:04.049
The poster has a hilarious B-movie vibe from
the 50s to 60s era when Luca is set and recalls

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:08.519
films such as Attack of the Crab Monsters
and Creature from the Black Lagoon.

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:12.930
In contrast to the much more appealing look
of Luca and Alberto's sea monsters, Uncle

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:17.610
Ugo was designed to be as scary and weird-looking
as possible for a Disney film, so that Luca

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:21.380
would dread the idea of having to go and live
with him in the trench.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:26.090
Casarosa has said Ugo is based on the very
strange creatures found in the deep;

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:30.910
and to me, with his bioluminescent lure, he
looks like a cross between an anglerfish like

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:35.980
the one we saw in Finding Nemo, and the Barreleye,
another weird-looking real-life fish that

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:40.410
has transparent flesh around its upper half
so you can see inside it, similar to Uncle

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:41.410
Ugo.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:46.230
By the way, in an interesting bit of casting,
Ugo is voiced by Sacha Baron Cohen, perhaps

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:51.310
best known for the Borat movies, and the odd
tone he uses adds to the uncomfortable effect

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.510
he has on Luca.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:54.610
"Hello, Luca.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:56.260
It's nice to..."

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:59.470
"Luca, I need you to punch his heart."

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:04.170
Luca also tips its hat to Finding Nemo via
the diving helmet both Alberto and Luca wear,

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.980
which is the same design as the one in Philip
Sherman's fish tank where Nemo ends up in

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:09.480
his movie.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.870
Luca's filmmakers also had some fun with the
surnames of the various sea monsters in the

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:14.870
movie.

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:19.540
So Luca's name "Paguro" is Italian for "hermit
crab", reflecting his sheltered life at the

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:24.350
beginning of the film and the reluctance of
his parents to let him venture above the surface.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:28.850
On top of that, it also foreshadows how Luca
will eventually come out of his shell and

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:32.760
reveal his true self as well as the fact that
ultimately he'll be comfortable living in

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.090
different surroundings than he grew up in.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:40.430
Alberto's surname "Scorfano" is Italian for
scorpion fish, and the Branzino family who

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:43.630
Luca's mum keeps mentioning get their name
from the Italian word for sea bass.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:44.630
"Let's see Bianca Branzino do that!"

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:52.880
And a hint to the secret that these two old
ladies are actually sea monsters is their

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.110
surname, "Aragosta", which is Italian for
lobster.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:06:00.200
Oh, and I also like the little detail that
the goatfish that Luca shepherds actually

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.770
bleat just like a land goat.
[bleating]

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:08.360
Director Enrico Casarosa has a couple of voice
cameos in Luca.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.300
He's the voice of the fisherman who sets up
the film's Stupido running joke when Alberto

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.430
and Luca overhear him shouting to a speeding
boat:

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:17.610
"What's wrong with you, Stupido?"

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:21.370
He's also the card player who yells "Scopa!"
when he wins.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.830
For those unfamiliar, that's a popular card
game played with an Italian deck of cards.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:30.170
You can see one of those designs close up
on the card that Alberto tries to steal at

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:33.080
the beginning of the movie and which Luca
later finds.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.520
And the reverse of the card with its picture
of a town in the top half reflected in the

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:42.770
lower half is a nice detail referencing the
underwater civilisation beneath the sea right

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:44.650
by Portorosso.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:48.710
When Luca and Alberto meet Giulia's father
Massimo for the first time, they discover

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:52.430
he has a particularly keen interest in hunting
sea monsters.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:56.670
The pictures pinned to his wall are a nod
to the various legends that Pixar's filmmakers

00:06:56.670 --> 00:07:02.220
researched for the movie, from old Renaissance
maps to legends of Japanese dragons and serpents.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:07.170
One particular legend from the town of Tellaro
in the Liguria region, tells of a giant octopus

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:11.810
that one night climbed out of the sea and
rang the church bells to warn the inhabitants

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:13.680
of an attack by pirates.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.090
And in Luca, there are octopus door knobs
as well as an octopus plaque near the town

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:22.730
square which reference similar ones from the
town of Tellaro that pay tribute to the legendary

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:24.210
octopus.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:28.991
And Octopuses, with their ability to camouflage
themselves and change the way they look, also

00:07:28.991 --> 00:07:33.970
inspired the filmmakers for how Luca's sea
monsters could quickly transform into humans.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:38.630
True to Luca's nostalgic feel and setting,
there are many easter eggs to the Italian

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:40.710
postwar Golden Age of cinema.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:45.870
There's the poster of La Strada, the Oscar-winning
drama by Fellini, and the name of the boat

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:49.120
"Gelsomina" is the name of the protagonist
in that movie.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:53.740
Casarosa also took inspiration from another
Fellini film, "I Vitelloni", for the final

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:54.900
scene of Luca.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.210
The design of the train that arrives to take
Luca away and how the scene is shot, with

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:04.080
Luca looking back to Alberto who runs after
him as the train departs, pays homage to the

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:05.890
ending of Fellini's film.

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:10.110
And if you were wondering what that black-and-white
photo is that Alberto looks at just before

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:15.030
he and Luca attempt their first ride down
the island slope, it's of the lead actor Marcello

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:19.230
Mastroianni from the Oscar-winning comedy-drama
Divorce Italian Style.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:24.230
And, fun fact, Chiara, his daughter with actress
Catherine Deneuve, lends her voice to Luca's

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:26.760
mum for the film's French-dubbed version.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:31.250
And another film starring Marcello Mastroianni
also pops up in the movie.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:35.419
Appropriately enough, the film playing on
the TV while Giulia and Luca are sneaking

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:41.840
across the rooftops is the 1958 acclaimed
Italian heist movie, Big Deal on Madonna Street.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:46.200
Another poster is for the Oscar-winning Roman
Holiday, a Hollywood romantic comedy set in

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:49.810
Rome and starring Gregory Peck and Audrey
Hepburn.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:54.400
Various signs in the town also pay homage
to other acclaimed Italian artists.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:59.580
The Vicolo De Sica references the neorealist
director Vittorio De Sica who directed classics

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.380
such as The Bicycle Thief.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:07.110
The Piazza Calvino is a nod to Italian writer
Italo Calvino, and Giulia and Massimo's surname

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:12.260
"Marcovaldo" is a shout-out to the name of
a short story collection penned by Calvino.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:17.080
And the Bar Pittaluga may be a reference to
the Italian film producer Stefano Pittaluga

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:21.610
who began his career as a cinema-owner in
Casarosa's home town of Genoa.

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:25.580
There's also what looks like a fake poster
which translates as "A Summer To Remember."

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:29.890
I think this poster is a fun reference to
how this movie is the story of a summer that

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:32.770
Luca, Alberto, and Giulia will never forget.

00:09:32.770 --> 00:09:38.500
Prior to directing Luca, Enrico Casarosa was
best known for his Oscar-nominated short film

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:42.690
La Luna, and the frequent lunar imagery in
this movie, together with Giulia and Luca's

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:46.550
fascination with astronomy, feel like a hat-tip
to the acclaimed short.

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:50.980
Plus, Giulia's father, Massimo, also has a
similar character design and thick, heavy

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:53.740
moustache as the father in the short film.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:57.390
And this boy drawing stars in the end credits
is the boy from La Luna.

00:09:57.390 --> 00:10:02.680
An in-joke that some coffee lovers might have
spotted is the bar sign for "LaTazza", referencing

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:07.740
not only the popular Italian brand of coffee,
Lavazza, but also the fact that "La Tazza"

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.230
means "cup" in Italian.

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:13.890
And I couldn't help but chuckle at all the
holy cheese variations that Giulia comes up

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.170
with whenever something surprises her.

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:17.470
"Santo Gorgonzola!

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:18.770
Santa mozzarella.

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:20.060
Santa ricotta.

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:21.360
Santo pecorino."

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.670
For the animation style of Luca, Casarosa
wanted to go with a more cartoonish, somewhat

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:31.529
exaggerated look that took inspiration from
both Looney Tunes and Miyazaki's films such

00:10:31.529 --> 00:10:32.980
as "The Castle of Cagliostro".

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:38.470
And you can see these influences in scenes
like where Alberto tries to ride his homemade

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:43.310
Vespa for the first time, and after it falls
apart, he ends up running down the hill, spinning

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:47.911
his legs so fast that extra limbs appears,
something you also see in this scene from

00:10:47.911 --> 00:10:49.960
The Castle of Cagliostro.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:54.780
And there's a Looney Tunes-inspired moment
when Luca's thrown off his bike after breaking

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:59.990
suddenly, and the halo of fish swimming above
his head is a suitably aquatic take on the

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:02.930
"circling birdies" effect often seen in cartoons.

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:07.680
Interestingly, Portorosso is a mash-up of
the names of several towns on the north-west

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:11.340
coast of Italy, where Luca's director spent
his own childhood.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:16.220
And according to Casarosa, a "wonderful extra
reason to name" the town that was Miyazaki's

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:18.279
film Porco Rosso, also a period film set in
Italy.

00:11:18.279 --> 00:11:22.500
There are some intriguing comparisons between
Luca and Disney's The Little Mermaid.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:27.330
Like Ariel, Luca wants to explore land-based
life above the surface, and both are told

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:29.680
not to go by their parents.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.130
Also like Ariel, Alberto collects objects
from the human world, and both films have

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:38.750
some amusing moments with forks, with the
sea creatures not properly understanding their

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:39.750
human use.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:44.170
And coincidentally, or perhaps not, Jacob
Tremblay who voices Luca will also be the

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:49.060
voice of Flounder in Disney's upcoming live-action
version of The Little Mermaid.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:52.480
When you get to the end of the movie, make
sure to sit through the credits as there's

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:56.480
a humorous post-credits scene which features
the return of Uncle Ugo.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:00.610
This time he's back in his original habitat
talking to what appears to be one of Luca's

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:03.310
goatfish about how great it is to live in
the deep.

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:07.200
"It's away from the hustle and bustle, all
the stuff that you don't want."

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:11.610
Ugo also eats a piece of whale carcass, a
call-back to how he described living in the

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:13.260
deep earlier in the movie.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:14.260
"Mmm.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:15.260
Whale carcass."

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:19.540
I'm curious to see how Luca fits in with the
Pixar theory first put forward by Jon Negroni

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:24.399
who also has a YouTube channel, and that theory
posits that after the events of WALL-E, animals

00:12:24.399 --> 00:12:27.440
evolved over time into the monsters from Monsters
Inc.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:31.500
In Luca, we can see that there's a species
of sea creature in the 1950s that already

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:35.200
has the ability to rapidly change into and
out of human form.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:38.700
If you've got any ideas, be sure to leave
them in the comments below, and if you want

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:43.490
to find out how Pixar's previous movie Soul
essentially confirmed many aspects of the

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:48.100
Pixar theory, tap here to watch my video on
that or follow the link in the video description.

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:52.950
And if like me, you really enjoyed the gorgeous
animation of Luca, you'll want to check out

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:56.110
the Art of Luca book with amazing concept
art from the movie.

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:59.480
I'll add a link where you can get a copy in
the description below.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:03.060
So, what were your favourite moments and easter
eggs in Luca?

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:06.910
If you enjoyed this, don't forget to like
and subscribe and you can tap left for my

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:10.600
next Pixar video or tap right for something
else you're sure to like.

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:12.810
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:14.130
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

